## Mandatory Code Patterns

### Business logic in services.py — never in views.py
```python
# views.py CORRECT
def cancel(self, request, pk):
    policy = get_object_or_404(Policy, pk=pk)
    result = PolicyService().cancel_policy(policy, reason=request.data.get("reason"))
    return Response(PolicySerializer(result).data)
```

### Kafka event schema — always this format
```python
{
    "event_id": str(uuid.uuid4()),
    "event_type": "policy.created",
    "occurred_at": timezone.now().isoformat(),
    "service": "policy-service",
    "data": { ... }
}
```

### Structured logs — request_id is injected automatically by RequestIDMiddleware
```python
# request_id is already bound to context via structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars()
# Do NOT pass it explicitly in HTTP views — middleware handles it
logger.info("policy_created", policy_id=str(policy.id), customer_id=str(policy.customer_id))

# Exception: Celery tasks and Kafka consumers (no HTTP context) — bind manually
logger.info("email_sent", notification_id=notification_id, request_id=task_request_id)
```

### Standard error format — all services
```json
{
  "error": { "code": "POLICY_CANCELLED", "message": "Business language, never technical.", "details": {} },
  "request_id": "uuid"
}
```

### Settings split by environment
```
config/settings/base.py | development.py | production.py | test.py
```
Always use `python-decouple` for env vars. Never hardcode.

